  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The 8(a) Business Development set-aside program obligated $7.9B in FY2026 to date, across 9,454 awards with an average award size of $834K. Obligations fell 42.4% year over year. The Department of Defense accounted for the largest share of buying agency obligations at $3.9B. Computer Systems Design Services led NAICS industries with $945M, followed by Other Computer Related Services at $826M and Facilities Support Services at $762M. The top vendors in FY2026 to date include AKIMA FACILITIES OPERATIONS LLC at $95.1M and CHEROKEE NATION STRATEGIC PROGRAMS, L.L.C. at $83.7M. Annual context shows obligations peaked at $15.1B in FY24 and then declined to $13.7B in FY25 and $7.9B in FY26 to date. These figures are contract obligations; FY2026 totals are partial and reflect fiscal year to date activity. This snapshot highlights concentration in defense buying, a strong representation of computer and facilities services, and a long tail of small awards by count.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did obligations change year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 42.4% year over year. The Department of Defense remained the largest buyer with $3.9B, but overall FY2026 to date totals were $7.9B compared with $13.7B in FY25 and $15.1B in FY24. Changes in procurement timing and the partial fiscal year totals can compress year-over-year comparisons. Agencies with smaller FY2026 to date totals include the Department of Homeland Security at $693M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $683M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is 8(a) spending concentrated?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense concentrated the largest share of obligations at $3.9B. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry by obligations at $945M, followed by Other Computer Related Services at $826M and Facilities Support Services at $762M. The largest individual contractors are AKIMA FACILITIES OPERATIONS LLC at $95.1M and CHEROKEE NATION STRATEGIC PROGRAMS, L.L.C. at $83.7M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does the federal government award through 8(a) set-asides?",
      "a": "The federal government awards $7.9B through 8(a) set-asides in FY2026 to date. The program produced 9,454 awards in FY2026 to date with an average award size of $834K. Obligations fell 42.4% year over year, and prior annual totals were $11.3B in FY21, $12.2B in FY22, $14.1B in FY23, $15.1B in FY24, and $13.7B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies use 8(a) set-asides the most?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense uses 8(a) set-asides the most, obligating $3.9B in FY2026 to date. Other top buying agencies in FY2026 to date include the Department of Homeland Security at $693M, the Department of Health and Human Services at $683M, and the General Services Administration at $321M. The remaining top agencies are the Department of State at $319M, the Department of Justice at $299M, and NASA at $254M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are the largest 8(a) set-aside contractors?",
      "a": "AKIMA FACILITIES OPERATIONS LLC is the largest 8(a) set-aside contractor with $95.1M in obligations in FY2026 to date. Other top contractors include CHEROKEE NATION STRATEGIC PROGRAMS, L.L.C. at $83.7M, ASRC FEDERAL FACILITIES LOGISTICS LLC at $80.1M, and ASRC FEDERAL TECHNOLOGY SOLUTIONS, LLC at $74.6M. The top ten vendors also list RSI-QUANTITECH JV LLC at $65M and AGILE DECISION SCIENCES, LLC at $50.3M."
    }
